- Branch protection has an overlooked bypass actor, and “administrators obey rules” was never enabled.
- An agent can edit the workflow that judges its own change, or can invoke an existing privileged workflow with attacker-controlled inputs.
- Repository or environment secrets are available to same-repository PR workflows even though fork behavior looked safe.
- Self-hosted runners retain credentials, processes, containers, caches, or modified toolchains between requests.
- A cache or artifact crosses the untrusted-verifier/publisher boundary and becomes an execution channel.
- Third-party Actions, container tags, package indexes, or system packages are mutable, making yesterday’s passing check irreproducible.
- “Same content” was interpreted as the same tree while a check actually depends on commit parents, commit message, author, timestamp, tags, or shallow-history depth.
- Server-side squash, rebase, automatic branch update, or merge-message editing changes the object after checks.
- Required status contexts can be forged by another workflow or App with checks permission.
- A status belongs to the PR head rather than the synthesized merge result.
- A source branch is force-pushed or deleted after intake; the underlying object later becomes unreachable.
- Scheduled workflows are delayed or silently disabled because of inactivity, billing, quota, or repository configuration.
- Concurrent retries are safe but create a CI storm; a long-running request starves indefinitely behind small changes.
- “Retry flaky tests” gradually becomes a mechanism for accepting genuinely nondeterministic failures.
- Test commands pass while modifying the working tree, generated files, lockfiles, or snapshots that are not part of the candidate.
- Git LFS objects, submodules, external schemas, or downloaded fixtures are mutable even though the Git tree is fixed.
- Linux and macOS disagree on case sensitivity, executable bits, symlinks, line endings, Unicode normalization, or generated output.
- Stacked changes land out of order, duplicate one another, or depend on commits not included in the request.
- Reverting a merge is not clean because later landings depend on it; “automatic rollback” needs dependency awareness.
- Request IDs are reused, truncated, or copied between repositories; updated content is mistaken for an idempotent retry.
- PR title, body, labels, approvals, or identity mapping remain mutable after checks and silently alter audit meaning.
- Commit trailers allow ambiguous escaping, duplicate keys, oversized values, secrets, personal data, or schema collisions.
- The audit parser assumes SHA-1 length, one merge strategy, or one receipt schema forever.
- Protected-path rules miss an indirect control surface: dependency lockfiles, build scripts, code generators, CODEOWNERS, reusable workflows, or test configuration.
- The emergency override becomes the convenient path and no longer receives equivalent receipts or retrospective scrutiny.
- Shared-host agents can inspect each other’s environment, Git credential helpers, sockets, process arguments, or filesystem despite repository-level isolation.
- GitHub API rate limits, Actions concurrency limits, storage retention, log truncation, or artifact expiry break recovery assumptions.
- A passing change is semantically wrong because no deterministic check captured the requirement. The gate guarantees process integrity, not program correctness.

## 4. Assumptions

- Agents are not entitled to write `main` directly. Does the real design remove that permission from every token and API path?
- The boss accepts rare approval for gate, policy, credential, and ownership changes. Which paths or settings require that approval?
- GitHub Actions or an equivalent hosted executor is available. What executes the gate if it is not?
- Clean, disposable verifier and publisher environments are available. Are any jobs self-hosted or workspace-reusing?
- The repository can support distinct planner, verifier, reporter, and publisher permission boundaries. How are those boundaries enforced?
- A protected publisher environment can be entered only by trusted default-branch workflow code. Has this been demonstrated adversarially?
- Agents may be buggy or opportunistic, so submitted code is treated as hostile. Is the intended threat model weaker or stronger?
- The shared Ubuntu host cannot safely hold a main-writing credential. Where are all current credentials stored and who can read them?
- Agent branch credentials may leak without compromising main. Are their scopes genuinely limited, including rules, checks, workflows, and merge APIs?
- Every task can use a separate clone or worktree. Are there tools that require a single shared working tree?
- A specific commit SHA is the unit of submission. Does the real workflow instead treat a mutable branch or PR head as authoritative?
- Merge commits are acceptable in main history. If squash or rebase is required, how is exact checked-object identity preserved?
- An empty envelope commit is acceptable. If not, where does immutable request metadata live?
- The request’s explanation is requester-supplied provenance, not independently verified truth. What does “why” need to mean for audit purposes?
- Git history must retain accepted receipts; rejected-attempt diagnostics may remain in GitHub. Must refusals also survive independently of GitHub retention?
- Checks can be expressed as deterministic commands with bounded resources. Which checks require external services, nondeterministic data, hardware, or model judgment?
- The policy from the current base governs a retry. Should requests instead retain the policy that existed when first submitted?
- Infrastructure failures may retry automatically, while genuine test failures require new immutable content or an explicit policy-approved rerun. How is flakiness classified?
- Merge conflicts are refused rather than model-resolved. Is automatic conflict resolution expected?
- Compare-and-swap retry cost is acceptable for a small fleet. What arrival rate and check duration define “small”?
- GitHub retains PRs and gate-owned refs reliably enough for in-flight recovery. What retention and cleanup requirements apply?
- Submodules, Git LFS, generated artifacts, and external dependencies are absent or can be pinned. Which of them exist?
- Agents cannot alter repository rules, install Apps, approve protected changes, or mint privileged tokens. Has the complete actor matrix been enumerated?
- The boss’s Mac credential is not accessible from the Ubuntu agent host. What is the recovery plan if that device or account is unavailable?
- A GitHub-wide outage may pause landing without a secondary system. What recovery-time objective is required?
- There is no production deployment coupled to main, as stated, so atomic Git publication is the only transaction boundary. Are any undocumented consumers triggered immediately by a main update?
- Reverts may use the same gate and need not preempt it. Does the boss require an emergency priority lane?
- Main history is not routinely rewritten. If rewriting is ever allowed, how are receipts and request identities preserved?

